          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Anxiety sits in the mental and behavioural chapter. Where would a physical symptom of it, like palpitations, be classified?",
          "answer": "**Anxiety** is classified in the mental and behavioral chapter. The anxiety, phobia, panic and stress-related categories sit in the F40 to F48 range, which is the block for non-psychotic mental disorders (ICD-10-CM Chapter 5).\n\n**Palpitations** are not in the circulatory disease chapter. They are in the symptoms chapter, Chapter 18 (R00 to R99), in the block for circulatory and respiratory symptoms (R00 to R09). The circulatory chapter notes that palpitations stay with the symptoms in Chapter 18 until a diagnosis is recorded (ICD-10-CM Chapter 9). I could not confirm the specific code R00.2 from the passages I retrieved, so check that code in the official tables.\n\nThe principle you mentioned is supported by that same circulatory-chapter passage. When no diagnosis explains a symptom, the symptom is coded on its own. My search for more detail on this convention failed, so I cannot say more from the sources. The sources also do not say how a physical symptom of anxiety is coded when a clinician has recorded anxiety as the diagnosis. Coding decisions like that depend on the clinician's documentation and the full coding rules.\n\nIf palpitations come with chest pain, fainting, or trouble breathing, seek emergency care now. This answer is educational and does not tell you what condition you have. A clinician can explain how a symptom is coded in your own records.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
